\section{Delisting returns}{

How delisting returns enter the data depends on \code{version}:
\itemize{
\item \code{"v2"} (CIZ): CRSP already includes the delisting return in the
monthly (\code{mthret}) and daily (\code{dlyret}) returns of the month and the
day in which a stock delists, so \code{ret} and \code{ret_excess} include it
without further adjustment. The common-stock screens of the security
history (\code{crsp.stksecurityinfohist}) are evaluated on the stock's last
trading day for these observations, because the history holds no
active record after it. Without this, the month or day that carries
the delisting return would be dropped.
\item \code{"v1"} (legacy SIZ), monthly: the delisting return \code{dlret} from
\code{crsp.msedelist} replaces the return of the delisting month in
\code{ret_adj}, which \code{ret_excess} is based on. If \code{dlret} is missing, the
return is set to -30\% for performance-related delistings (codes 500,
520, 551-574, 580, and 584; Shumway, 1997), kept for code 100, and set
to -100\% otherwise. \code{ret} is the unadjusted return.
\item \code{"v1"} (legacy SIZ), daily: the delisting return replaces the
return on the delisting date, and later observations are dropped.
}
}

